@node "FPGA" "FPGA emulation?"
@{JCENTER}@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/32seonliner.png"}

Short answer is NO, FPGA is not emulation

Bear with me, I'm not a PRO !
@{JLEFT}
               I've seen a bit everywhere even in Amiga mags
               that writer claims FPGA is emulation... WTF
               Even some claims FPGA runs on Linux...
               How can anybody write about a subject without 
               atleast some small knowledge?!...

               In computing, emulation is the process where one
               computer system (the host) mimics the hardware or
               software behavior of another system (the guest)

               FPGA-based hardware recreation is a method of using
               a Field-Programmable Gate Array (FPGA) chip to
               physically reconfigure its internal logic blocks
               and wiring to mimic the hardware architecture of
               another electronic system
          
               So it's not emulation, what is it? It's hardware
               just like any chip in your Amiga, if it's done
               right it can replicate a chip with 100% accuracy
          
          
               If you look at MiSTer FPGA, it has Linux(ARM cores)
               and it's used to select computer model to run 
               Loads the FPGA with selected core, Linux does not 
               run the recreated computer model. Lately this seems
               to change a bit, new systems like new BigMig Amiga
               core with 3DX2 takes out the 68k CPU from the FPGA
               and replaces it with 3DX2 core and 68k is instead
               emulated on the second ARM core, bare metal no 
               Linux.

               The 3D card follows the same split. A 3D engine in 
               the firmware, on the ARM, takes a program's 
               triangles, clips them and sorts them into bands of
               the screen. In the FPGA, two render units (the "x2")
               each pair a triangle-setup unit written for BigMig
               with a pixel pipeline

               I suspect 68k needs to be fast for 3DX2 is to be
               usefull. It's same rule in PC world, you pair
               CPU/GPU for best performance or you end up with a
               lipstick on a pig ;)

               MiSTer's FPGA is very versatile since it also has a
               ARM cores in it, Apollo Vampire V4 has a similair 
               FPGA but does not contain a ARM core

@{JCENTER}




I'll try explaining FPGA in a easy way as I can...

@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/and4.gif"}
This is a AND gate

@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/or4.gif"}
OR gate

@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/nand4.gif"}
NAND gate

@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/nor5.gif"}
NOR gate
 
@{JLEFT} 
               What I'm showing you is the very basic function in
               a chip, different gates when connected together in
               a massive layout gives you the desired funtion of
               a chip like AGA Lisa, 20000 gates / 80000 transistors
               
               Above gates the basic ones has 2 transistors per gate
               
                               let's keep it simple


 
@{JCENTER}@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/brfov.jpg"}
This is a basic function in a FPGA, called LUT,
look-up table (there are many more other types)
@{JLEFT} 

          The LUT is actually a small SRAM memory 40 transistors

          Instead of calculating the logic using physical gates,
          a LUT looks up the answer in a tiny, programmable truth
          table memory array

          If the FPGA software flashes 0, 0, 0, 1 into that LUT's 
          memory, it behaves exactly like an AND gate.

          If it flashes 0, 1, 1, 1, the exact same hardware instantly
          becomes an OR gate.

          You see a 40 transistor LUT does a 2 transistors job, but
          it's veratile and this is the cost
          
          When you write hardware description code (Verilog/VHDL) to 
          replicate a chip, you don't manually choose which LUT 
          connects to which wire.

          Instead, you write a Timing Constraint file (SDC or XDC).
          You tell the compiler: "My system clock is exactly 100 MHz."
          The compiler's Static Timing Analysis (STA) tool then goes 
          to work during compilation:

          1. It calculates the exact nanosecond delay of every chosen
             routing path and LUT.
   
          2. It ensures Setup Time is met: The data arrives at the 
             destination early enough

          3. It ensures Hold Time is met: The data stays stable long
             enough

          4. If a path is too slow, the Place & Route engine physically
             rips up the wires and moves the LUTs closer together until
             the timing requirement is mathematically proven to pass.


          Conclusion
               
          You flash a FPGA with a "layout" bitstream.
               
          Switch power on the FPGA clears all SRAM cells and pulls the
          bitstream out of the Flash chip
               
          As those millions of 1s and 0s stream into the FPGA, they fill
          up thousands of internal configuration memory cells. 
               
          Once the entire bitstream is safely loaded and verified, the
          FPGA becomes active


          

                            I tried to keep it simple





          
          
          Now you wonder can FPGA replicate anything? no FPGAs are slow
          in comparison to a modern CPU/GPU and the amount of gates 
          needed for that.
          
          FPGA suites best for low clock and not too big "layouts"
          Expensive High-end modern FPGA can in theory hit 800-900 MHz


                  I hope it was not too deep and maybe it shed
                  some light into your knowledge pool ;)
                  
                  
                      Sorry if there is any fact errors !



@{JCENTER}
@{FG shine}
*** ***
*********
**** ****
*******
*****
*
@{FG text}



@endnode     
       
       


@node "A500" "Amiga 500 Part III"
@{JCENTER}@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/32seonliner.png"}

Part III "NEW Amiga 500"

@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/500-2.jpeg"}
My aim is one computer for all and it's a mATX build and in
a simple way to bring more Amiga feeling to it.

To be clear, this is my "PC" running Linux CachyOS with FS-UAE


The 3D stuff around Raspberry Pi, PiStorm3D got my interest..

Can I have it on my custom A500 build?

.

.

.

.

.

HELL YEAH !!!!

.

.
@{JLEFT}
                         ____   _____  _____ __  __ 
                        |___ \\ |  __ \\|  ___|\\ \\/ / 
                          __) || |  | | |_    \\  /  
                         |__ < | |  | |  _|   /  \\  
                         ___) || |__| | |    /_/\\_\\ 
                        |____/ |_____/|_| or whatever 

@{JCENTER}
QuarkTexNG gives WinUAE 3D acceleration, but I'm using FS-UAE

I noticed that JIT is not enabled on the build and no 80bit math
So I recompiled one with these 2 features set...

Aminet/QuarkTexNG.lha, I took Warp3D, agl, minigl library files
and put them in AmigaOS Libs: folder

FS-UAE also needs a .so plugin for direct 3D gfx access...
I recompiled WinUAE version for FS-UAE ;) so full name is
quarktexng-linux-x86-64.so and put this to plugin folder of FS-UAE

Now we are almost there....

FS-UAE launcher

click the cog icon (1th tab) where you can add custom settings, 
add following...

uae_compfpu = 1        
uae_cpu060_revision = 6
uae_fpu_softfloat = 0  
uae_native_code = 1    
use_cachesize = 8192   

close Custom Configuration window

5th tab

Zorro III Fast Memory, I have 512 MB. I think minimum 128 MB
Graphics Card RAM: 64 MB

Last tab

CPU 68060-NOMMU
JIT Compiler enabled


This is important setting for performance, ofcource you need
UAEGFX etc. enabled but I think your not new to this stuff!





SYSINFO, it shows there is some potential speed in the system.

@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/a3d1.png"}


I ran the minigl gear test from Aminet/MiniGL_Library_Classic.lha
DO NOT USE THE minigl.library IN THIS ARCHIVE

When I took the screenshot it missed the fps numbers,
it was so fast, about 1040fps ;)

@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/a3d2.png"}


And now a game test ;)

.

.

.

.

OpenLara MiniGL for AmigaOS 3 by Arcziii

https://arcziii.itch.io/tomb-raider, DL both files

In OpenLara-MiniGL folder put audio-adp4 folder with files
Then grab PC version of Tomb Raider (1) and copy the Data folder
and put it in OpenLara-MiniGL folder.

You can edit OpenLara icon and change resolution and fullscreen mode

@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/tr1.png"}

Game running in 1920x1080 Fullscreen setting around 48-49fps !





@{" Show zip archive " SYSTEM "c:execute 3d"}

It contains all the files you need + MiniGL_Library_Classic so you
can run the demos files(4) and see you got the system working ;)

You might wan't to check Aminet if there is never files before 
jumping into it, also the .so plugin might not work with never
version of QuarkTexNG

fs-uae.jit or fs-uae.3d, rename and copy to /usr/bin in Linux

fs-uae.3d   this one is for the 3D & JIT                              
fs-uae.jit  this only enables jit feature, no 80bit match if you don't
care about 3D and still want the JIT feature                          





I'm no PRO at this stuff, research and patience and plenty of 6-packs ;)





I did another game test Doom 64 EX+ from https://arcziii.itch.io/doom64

Also in 1920x1080, run smooth no lag. I don't know if there is a way to
get a fps counter but who cares it's just a number most important is how
it plays! 


@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/d1.png"}
@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/d2.png"}
@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/d3.png"}


